  "question": {
    "text": "Consider the following standard electrode potentials (<math><msup><mrow><mtext>E</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>o</mtext></mrow></msup></math> in volts) in aqueous solution:<br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtable><mtr><mtd><munder><mrow><mtext>E</mtext><mtext>l</mtext><mtext>e</mtext><mtext>m</mtext><mtext>e</mtext><mtext>n</mtext><mtext>t</mtext></mrow><mrow></mrow></munder></mtd><mtd><munder><mrow><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mtext>M</mtext></mrow><mrow></mrow></munder></mtd><mtd><munder><mrow><msup><mtext>M</mtext><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mtext>M</mtext></mrow><mrow></mrow></munder></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mtext>A</mtext><mtext>l</mtext></mtd><mtd><mo>-</mo><mn>1.66</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>+</mo><mn>0.55</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mtext>T</mtext><mtext>l</mtext></mtd><mtd><mo>+</mo><mn>1.26</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>-</mo><mn>0.34</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable></math><br />Based on these data, which of the following statements is correct?",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "fLjRT",
      "text": "<math><mtext>T</mtext><msup><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> is more stable than <math><mtext>A</mtext><msup><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "K4TV0",
      "text": "<math><mtext>A</mtext><msup><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>  is more stable than  <math><mtext>A</mtext><msup><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "KAjol",
      "text": "<math><mtext>T</mtext><msup><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> is more stable than <math><mtext>A</mtext><msup><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "JcXTn",
      "text": "<math><mtext>T</mtext><msup><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> is more stable than <math><mtext>A</mtext><msup><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<math><mo>&#8710;</mo><msup><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">G</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mtext></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mtext><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mtext><msup><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mtext></mrow></msup></math><br /><br />if <math><msup><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mtext></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mtext><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mtext><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mtext><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mtext><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mtext><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mtext><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mtext><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">v</mtext><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mtext><mo>;</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mtext></math>then <math><mo>&#8710;</mo><msup><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">G</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mtext></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mtext><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mtext><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mtext><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mtext><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mtext><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mtext><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">v</mtext><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mtext></math>,<br /><br />For those, conversions whose <math> <mrow> <msup> <mtext>E</mtext> <mtext>o</mtext> </msup> </mrow> </math> values are more positive are more favourable.<br />As we can see from then electrode potential data, that reduction of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Al</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math>&nbsp;is spontaneous, but reduction of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Tl</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math>&nbsp;is not. So, we can conclude from this that <math><mtext>T</mtext><msup><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> is more stable than <math><mtext>A</mtext><msup><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>&nbsp;",
